body:
Here is an example of where this easily causes a bug. I would like to assume that this is in some form a quite common pattern.

```js
const rec = db.getRecord(blockId)
pipeline(
  req, 
  fs.createWriteStream(blockPath, { flags: 'wx' })
    .on('open', () => {
      // Oops, this can be called after rec.destroy() :/.
      rec.set({
        path: blockPath
      })
    }),
  err => {
    rec.destroy()
    callback(err)
  }
)
```

issue.body:
Related to https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/23133.

Even if we change so that `open` is emitted before `destroy`. `pipeline` will not wait for completed destruction for all streams. Which can cause subtle bugs with things like:

```js
const rec = db.getRecord(blockId)
pipeline(
  req, 
  fs.createWriteStream(blockPath, { flags: 'wx' })
    .on('open', () => {
      // Oops, this can be called after rec.destroy() :/. I didn't expect that.
      rec.set({
        path: blockPath
      })
    }),
  err => {
    rec.destroy()
    callback(err)
  }
)
```
